{"connection_id": "BC-fc5214c4f11ea5b2673f", "ref": "Augustine, City of God 21.3", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "eternal punitive death without extinction", "note": "Augustine argues that the damned can endure punitive death without being annihilated; this gives a Christian account of the continuing, non-living condition the commentary marks in 87:13."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fcb191e1c53d499e2265", "ref": "Gospel of Thomas 19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Paradise trees and no taste of death", "note": "The saying describes unchanging trees in Paradise and promises that those who know them will not taste death; this is a concrete apocryphal image of the immortality sought in the commentary's false tree offer, recast as a blessed promise."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8dee42cd037af00345c2", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.42", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable body raised imperishable", "note": "Resurrection is sown perishable and raised imperishable; it gives a bodily account of the enduring life set over against death in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6a85be650cc52f9357b1", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.54", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Mortality clothed with immortality", "note": "Paul's resurrection scene says the mortal must put on immortality and death is swallowed up in victory; it gives the hereafter's enduring life a concrete bodily endpoint unlike the fire's suspended condition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4dd3d970b965787ec683", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.15.55", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Hosea's death taunt in resurrection", "note": "Paul quotes the death-and-Sheol taunt associated with Hosea to celebrate resurrection's victory; this Christian reading turns the Hebrew prophet's ambiguous challenge into an assured defeat of death, unlike the commentary's unrelieved punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ff07e2e0996281fdc4df", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "World passes, God's will remains", "note": "John says the world and its desire are passing, while the one who does God's will remains forever; this closely specifies the commentary's near worldly life versus the more enduring hereafter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-54c751cbe4ddf2130141", "ref": "SBLGNT:1John.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "world passes away, God-doer abides forever", "note": "The world and its desire are passing away, while the one who does God's will abides forever; this is a close formulation of transient worldly life against lasting life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0ad5a80ae26ebaf2cd9", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Imperishable inheritance kept in heaven", "note": "Peter describes an inheritance in heaven that cannot perish, spoil, or fade; this specifies what enduring future good means beside the commentary's contrast with the near world."}
{"connection_id": "BC-366ffcdaa3c4fe6c6c37", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.4.13", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "believers who sleep and hope beyond grief", "note": "Paul calls the deceased \"those who sleep\" and contrasts believers' grief with those without hope; this Christian death-as-sleep idiom supplies the recoverable horizon that the commentary denies to the fire-dweller."}
