{"connection_id": "BC-525380fa073c220e53a1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "river of water of life in the final paradise", "note": "John's final city contains a river of the water of life flowing from God's throne, a directly corresponding paradise-water image for the commentary's life-giving water."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9b4b75a336408d850d7b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Leaves for healing", "note": "The Paradise river's tree bears leaves for healing the nations; beside the commentary's water that revives anything it touches, Revelation depicts life-giving restoration extending through the whole blessed scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ab7f035623f610efbd4b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.22.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tree of life and leaves for healing", "note": "Beside the river stands the tree of life whose leaves heal the nations; it brings the commentary's life-water and immortality-tree images together in the final garden."}
{"connection_id": "BC-799ccf0f18ec002308a5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "reputation of life but actual death", "note": "The Sardis church has a name of being alive but is dead; this non-biological use of life and death concretely parallels the commentary's definition of life by active goodness rather than mere survival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d3b1b9ca68b8e305716", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.7.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "waters of life and tears removed", "note": "The Lamb guides the redeemed to springs of waters of life and God wipes every tear; it combines the commentary's paradise water that gives life with an eschatological relief opposite fire-torment."}

{"connection_id": "BC-7f2094d26181482d0407", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.9.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "death sought but cannot be found", "note": "Under locust torment people seek death and cannot find it; like the fire-dweller of 87:13, torment continues while the normal exit of death is withheld."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e4f3617559ac64a7c074", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.6.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Death versus eternal life", "note": "Paul sets sin's wage, death, against God's gift of eternal life; the opposed outcomes make explicit the moral choice between death and lasting life that frames the commentary's contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c6195d822a6fdc7bb848", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.6.23", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sin's wage death, God's gift eternal life", "note": "Paul pairs sin's wage of death with God's gift of eternal life, a moral and eschatological life/death contrast that extends the commentary's link between life, goodness, and final outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ba32e610743841740a48", "ref": "Tertullian, Apology 48", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "eternal fire that punishes without consuming", "note": "Tertullian's final-judgment account treats eternal fire as ongoing punishment rather than a fire that simply consumes its victims, a patristic counterpart to the commentary's persistent fire; the precise translation should be verified."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd30a86a76ac28875f5a", "ref": "2 Baruch 51:3-5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Righteous transformed to lasting glory", "note": "At resurrection the righteous are changed into splendor and live in an enduring order while the wicked wither; this gives apocalyptic detail to the commentary's contrast between the hereafter and the fate of those who reject it."}
{"connection_id": "BC-80c1349b0c5d2b4bd852", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "No resurrection to life for the tyrant", "note": "The martyr says that for the persecuting king there will be no resurrection to life; this gives the commentary's contrast between the condemned state and lasting life a specific judgment scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c5d1eed990a5333f2887", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "resurrection to life denied to the persecutor", "note": "The martyr tells the king that he will have no resurrection to life, a direct two-fate response to tyrannical violence that sharpens the commentary's contrast between punishment and the purified person's recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f198e66fac345ebebe29", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Renewal of life after death", "note": "A tortured brother accepts death while trusting that the King of the universe will raise him to renewed life; the scene concretely shows why the better lasting life can be preferred over survival now."}
{"connection_id": "BC-97231ee6e72149c925b9", "ref": "2 Maccabees 7:9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tyrant's torture and everlasting renewal of life", "note": "A tortured brother tells the king that the King of the universe will raise the faithful to an everlasting renewal of life; like the commentary's Pharaoh-and-magicians scene, a ruler's bodily threat is answered by appeal to durable divine life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f99470b0e474b6875fd5", "ref": "4 Ezra 7:36", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "furnace of Gehenna opposite paradise of delight", "note": "4 Ezra displays the furnace of Gehenna opposite the place of rest and paradise of delight, a Jewish apocalyptic pairing that makes the commentary's fire and true-life alternatives into adjacent final scenes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e570aaa3cb0aa24ba2cf", "ref": "Berakhot 17a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "world to come without bodily needs, righteous enjoy divine splendor", "note": "The rabbinic description removes eating, drinking, and other bodily satisfactions from the world to come while the righteous enjoy divine splendor; it offers a specific Jewish construal of the commentary's true life as more than ordinary worldly vitality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db19207ad2fc1252166e", "ref": "Berakhot 18a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Righteous living, wicked dead", "note": "The commentary distinguishes life as benefit and good from mere biological existence; the Talmud reads “the living know they will die” so the righteous are called living even in death and the wicked dead even while alive, turning the life/death terms into moral evaluations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79f1126aad7d65340ba1", "ref": "Berakhot 18a", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "righteous called living in death, wicked dead in life", "note": "The rabbinic maxim calls the righteous living even in death and the wicked dead even while alive; it makes life and death moral-spiritual categories, a useful interpretive comparison for the commentary's non-biological definition of life as good and benefit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8311928201d1ab5d2152", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "This world as vestibule", "note": "The Mishnah pictures this world as a vestibule from which one prepares to enter the world to come; this interprets present life as provisional, clarifying why the commentary calls the hereafter the better lasting choice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5f7dfe9489152c708fb", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "this world as corridor before the world to come", "note": "Avot calls this world a corridor before the world to come and urges preparation here; its spatial-temporal image closely frames the commentary's near worldly life as preparatory rather than abiding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a62ac85209f559e760f6", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "World-to-come outweighs this life", "note": "The Mishnah explicitly says one hour of bliss in the world to come is better than all the life of this world; this is the closest Jewish formulation of the commentary's claim that the hereafter is better and more enduring than preferred worldly life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-855ed9cd8f8c6566d9f5", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "one hour in this world and one hour in the world to come", "note": "Avot says both that repentance and good deeds in this world outweigh all life in the world to come and that one hour of bliss there outweighs all life here; its two-sided valuation complicates a simple worldly-life versus afterlife ranking."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a3f759c29c18fb962f5a", "ref": "Mishnah Eduyot 2:10", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "ordinary Gehinnom judgment limited to twelve months", "note": "Mishnah Eduyot limits the ordinary judgment of the wicked in Gehinnom to twelve months; that rabbinic duration qualifies the commentary's image of remaining in fire without death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8cc7b0c46088aae600b9", "ref": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 10:1", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "shares and exclusions in the world to come", "note": "The mishnah grants all Israel a share in the world to come while listing exclusions; it gives a Jewish interpretive map of who receives enduring future life, relevant to the commentary's final division without claiming the same criteria."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a967ac00a0213bcfcf87", "ref": "Targum Jonathan on Isaiah 66:24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "undying souls in Gehinnom", "note": "The Targum turns Isaiah's corpse-and-fire scene into punishment in Gehinnom in which the sinners' souls do not die, an interpretive move that closely approaches the commentary's deathless fire."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2eabf352e9f76d641433", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.2.6", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God kills, makes alive, lowers and raises", "note": "Hannah's hymn says that the Lord kills and makes alive and brings down to Sheol and raises; it gives a fuller scriptural pattern of divine control over the two poles named in the commentary."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cede1ac7f1b8e9dd38e5", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Awakening and everlasting life", "note": "Daniel's sleepers in the dust awake to divergent outcomes, including everlasting life; this supplies the afterlife distinction that the commentary uses to contrast worldly life with the hereafter and its condemned endpoint."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6ed3e375d7484f88c341", "ref": "WLC:Dan.12.2", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sleepers in dust awaken to enduring fates", "note": "Those sleeping in dust awaken, some to everlasting life and some to disgrace; it makes the commentary's sleep, life, and lasting afterlife distinctions into two final outcomes rather than the suspended state of 87:13."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7c37eca9043b54160920", "ref": "WLC:Dan.3.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "faithful people before a king's fiery threat", "note": "The three Judeans tell the king that their God can rescue them from the blazing furnace and from his hand; this supplies a scriptural tyrant, fire, and faithful confession beside Pharaoh's threatened believers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d0f7535de07f16423cca", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "everlasting dominion that will not pass away", "note": "The Son of Man receives a dominion that is everlasting and will not pass away; it supplies a divinely granted, genuinely imperishable rule in contrast to the commentary's satanic promise of a kingdom that does not wear out."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8e374266380fda8bf49b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.20.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "not leaving any breathing thing alive", "note": "The ban commands that no breathing thing be left alive, using survival as the opposite of total extirpation; this gives a concrete Hebrew Bible counterpart to the commentary's remaining/sparing sense of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-954428f82a1fb8aa5f0b", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "source label ح ي ي B013; life and good versus death and evil", "note": "Moses places life and good over against death and evil; Hebrew חַיִּים is cognate with the source label ح ي ي, and the explicit life/good pairing closely supports the commentary's secondary definition of life as benefit and goodness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e3a66417428074ab4679", "ref": "WLC:Deut.30.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חיים, life", "note": "Moses urges Israel to choose life so that they and their offspring may live; the Hebrew life cognate matches the ayah's life vocabulary, though its immediate setting is covenantal survival in the land rather than the commentary's hereafter."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c71619ffbe8379bd091", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.39", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God kills and gives life", "note": "The commentary closes by citing Abraham's claim that God kills and then gives life; Deuteronomy puts that same power in God's own speech, making life and death dependent on God rather than on a ruler's threat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-efc787b248cb68b72640", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.39", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God kills and makes alive", "note": "God declares, \"I kill and I make alive\"; this is a direct Hebrew Bible formulation for the commentary's claim that Abraham assigns both death and life to the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d1ee39bdffc39e6410f1", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Living and dead as distinct states", "note": "Qohelet contrasts the living, who know they will die, with the dead, who know nothing; against that clean division, the commentary stresses the disturbing condition that is neither death nor beneficial life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9e46e971f1225c50aac8", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.5", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the dead know nothing and have no further reward", "note": "Qohelet says the dead know nothing and no longer have reward; within its under-the-sun argument, this is a sharp Hebrew Bible counterpoint to the commentary's afterlife as the locus of true life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-924500c1fee6d1c5b9e9", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.16", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "source label ح ي ي B006; daughters are to be kept alive", "note": "Pharaoh orders the midwives to kill a Hebrew son but let a daughter live; it is the biblical Pharaoh setting that makes the commentary's \"leave your women alive\" formula a concrete survival claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9a94cbfb914abdfce4f0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.1.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חיה, let live", "note": "Pharaoh orders that every daughter be kept alive (תְּחַיּוּן); the Hebrew life verb matches the commentary's cited Arabic gloss of “let live” as “keep them alive,” including the survival sense rather than mere vitality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-47d6f33a9bb7fc997551", "ref": "WLC:Exod.8.15", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Egyptian magicians name God's finger while Pharaoh hardens", "note": "Moses' Egyptian magicians identify the plague as God's finger, but Pharaoh remains hard; the shared Moses-magicians-Pharaoh scene differs from the commentary's magicians who openly believe and receive Pharaoh's threat."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84f9ca06af6394a04fd2", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.37.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God's Spirit put within the dead so they live", "note": "God promises to put his spirit within the restored people so that they live; this specifies divine reanimation by a life-giving force, the positive opposite of the fire's absence of life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b48bdc431a6da0adf73c", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.47.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "River trees heal", "note": "Ezekiel's temple river nourishes trees whose leaves heal; alongside the commentary's Paradise water that revives whatever it touches, this supplies the complementary healing effect of the life-giving waterscape."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd1c6ef0086e16865b71", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.47.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "River makes creatures live", "note": "The commentary adds the Paradise water that revives whatever it touches; Ezekiel's temple river heals its waters and makes living creatures live wherever the stream goes, a concrete life-giving-water image."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f836b033f8fc451bcf96", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.47.9", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "water makes everything live where the river goes", "note": "Every living creature lives wherever Ezekiel's eschatological river goes; this is a close prophetic counterpart to the commentary's paradise water that vivifies whatever it reaches."}
{"connection_id": "BC-52562a1ee2bfcf3c1ea0", "ref": "WLC:Gen.2.17", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Death warning before the serpent's claim", "note": "The commentary recalls Satan's offer of a tree of immortality; God first warns that eating brings death, the particular claim the serpent then denies, making this neighboring verse necessary to read the promise as counterfeit."}

{"connection_id": "BC-1a7134ef32fd38404bc5", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Tree and living forever", "note": "The commentary calls the Satanic offer a tree of immortality and an imperishable realm; Genesis says the human might eat from the tree of life and live forever, giving the Eden scene its own guarded version of the desired permanence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dd42a27cc4c2b271be2b", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "חיה, live", "note": "Genesis uses the Hebrew life root in its statement that eating from the tree could make the human live forever; this is a direct cognate to يَحْيَىٰ in the commentary's contrast between life and death."}
{"connection_id": "BC-aec8fb8313919da2cc1f", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.22", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tree of life and living forever", "note": "God bars the human from eating the tree of life and living forever; its actual tree-and-everlasting-life scene provides the closest biblical counterpart to the commentary's deceptive tree of immortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7993af5c46b5ffde3b7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The way to the life tree barred", "note": "After the promise of living forever, Genesis stations guardians at the way to the tree of life; this adds the scene's denial of humanly seized permanence beside the commentary's false offer of an imperishable kingdom."}
{"connection_id": "BC-43571b3c8b762d4dccc9", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Serpent denies death", "note": "The commentary treats Satan's promise of the tree of immortality as counterfeit permanence; in Genesis the serpent directly contradicts the death warning, so the promised escape from death is itself suspect."}
{"connection_id": "BC-738f3e9d3f81bf086914", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "מות, death", "note": "The serpent's denial uses the Hebrew root מות for dying, a concrete cognate of the ayah's يَمُوتُ; here the word is a denied consequence of eating, sharpening the contrast with the condemned person who cannot die."}
{"connection_id": "BC-05a93af1ab5e3b3df3b4", "ref": "WLC:Gen.3.4", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "serpent promises no death", "note": "The serpent tells the woman that she will not surely die; this is the Torah's direct temptation by a denial of death beside the commentary's satanic offer of a tree of immortality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-28d3cec932a6e1fffe0e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.7", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "source label ب ق ي B003; preserve a remnant and keep alive", "note": "Joseph says God sent him ahead to preserve a remnant and keep the family alive; this joins remaining after threatened extinction to living, precisely the relation developed under the commentary's remaining/sparing branch."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a68475b5d1ef41891a1", "ref": "WLC:Hos.13.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Ransom from Death and Sheol", "note": "Hosea voices Death and Sheol as powers from which Israel may be ransomed, echoing the commentary's claim that God alone gives life after death; the Hebrew can be read as a threat as well as a promise, so the connection should retain that uncertainty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-acb7eb7c3d18058edfe7", "ref": "WLC:Hos.13.14", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Sheol and death in a disputed redemption formula", "note": "Hosea speaks of ransoming from Sheol and redeeming from death, but the grammar and immediate judgment context make its force disputed; its later life-over-death reception remains a cautious counterpart to the commentary's final death/life contrast."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f46201f01801fbe8860", "ref": "WLC:Isa.25.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Death swallowed forever", "note": "Isaiah promises that God will swallow death forever and wipe away tears; this is an affirmative restoration in which death ends and life remains, a sharp contrast to the commentary's fire where neither death nor life brings relief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-533a0a4e38aedea35c13", "ref": "WLC:Isa.25.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death swallowed forever and tears wiped away", "note": "God swallows up death forever and wipes tears from faces; this is a prophetic final reversal of the commentary's place where death does not provide release and true life is absent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-382cf7c1c22c3ff30300", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Dead awakened to life", "note": "Isaiah addresses the dead as rising and waking from dust, whereas the commentary's damned person has neither death's release nor life's benefit; this is a specific prophetic reversal of that suspended fate."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8ae51ffcfaf43b3001d9", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "מות and חיה in one reversal", "note": "Isaiah uses cognates for “your dead” and “will live” in a single resurrection claim; the same death/life vocabulary in the ayah is here joined affirmatively rather than negated."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b0e8da17014b82d91c36", "ref": "WLC:Isa.26.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "the dead live, dust-dwellers awake", "note": "Isaiah calls the dead to live and the dust-dwellers to awake and sing; this eschatological reversal is the positive opposite of the commentary's fire where neither death concludes nor life begins."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d553d90601cbe57c98a7", "ref": "WLC:Isa.38.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "The living praise God", "note": "Hezekiah says the living, unlike those descended to the pit, give thanks to God; this concretely illustrates the commentary's gloss of life as benefit and good, though its setting is recovery from illness rather than final judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5dfdedd506f1d847c7d2", "ref": "WLC:Isa.38.19", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "the living thank God", "note": "Hezekiah says that the living, the living give thanks to God; it makes active grateful goodness a mark of life, supporting the commentary's claim that bare survival without benefit or good is not life in the full sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3562fdedca0f96e7341b", "ref": "WLC:Isa.40.8", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fading grass and the word standing forever", "note": "Grass withers and the flower fades, but God's word stands forever; this concrete transient/enduring contrast supports the commentary's evaluation of the near life against what remains."}
{"connection_id": "BC-37bf8bb3f765d6c29412", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Undying worm and unquenched fire", "note": "The commentary describes people in fire who neither die nor live; Isaiah's closing image has the rebels' corpses exposed to a worm that does not die and fire that is not quenched, a related but carefully different image of unending judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5876950bb44f60680dc9", "ref": "WLC:Isa.66.24", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "corpses, undying worm, unquenched fire", "note": "Isaiah calls the rebels corpses, while the worm does not die and the fire is not quenched; that allocation differs from the commentary's conscious fire-inhabitant who is neither dead nor alive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5f654b40a970b8ce2586", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.12", "target": "sec9", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "death as sleep without awakening", "note": "A human being lies down and does not rise, not waking or being roused from sleep until the heavens are no more; Job supplies the unrecovered sleep/death image whose absence is developed in the commentary."}
